The Human-English constructs a domain \(V\) with a top and then deletes that top to obtain a universal domain \(U\). The tokens of \(V\) are two atomic objects \(\Delta\) and \(\nabla\), distinct from every ordered pair, together with the left copies \((X,\Delta)\) and the right copies \((\Delta,Y)\) for \(X,Y\in D_V\). Every finite set of these tokens is consistent. Entailment has four clauses: (3) every set entails \(\Delta\); (4) a set entails \(\nabla\) when \(\nabla\) belongs to it or both pair-projections entail \(\nabla\); (5) a set entails \((X',\Delta)\) when \(\nabla\) belongs to it or the left projection entails \(X'\); (6) the symmetric clause for \((\Delta,Y')\). Under that relation \(\Delta\), \((\Delta,\Delta)\), \(((\Delta,\Delta),\Delta)\), and so on are equivalent, and no other token is equivalent to \(\nabla\). Scott says \(V\) satisfies \(V\cong V\times V\), one solution among many. \(U\) is defined by (7) \(D_U=D_V\setminus\{\nabla\}\), (8) the same bottom, (9) the finite subsets of \(D_U\) that do not \(V\)-entail \(\nabla\), and (10) the restriction of \(V\)-entailment to those sets and tokens, so combinations such as \((\nabla,\Delta)\) remain. He reads a token as an exclusion of paths in the infinite binary tree, identifies the total elements of \(U\) with the infinite paths and the elements of \(U\) with the open sets of Cantor space except the whole space, and states without proof that every countably based information system \(\mathbf{A}\) is a retract of \(\mathbf{U}\) by approximable maps \(a\) and \(b\) with \(b\circ a=I_{\mathbf{A}}\) and \(a\circ b\subseteq I_{\mathbf{U}}\), hence that \(\mathbf{U}\to\mathbf{U}\) is a retract of \(\mathbf{U}\) and \(\mathbf{U}\) carries a \(\lambda\)-model. `VToken` is inductive, with constructors `bot`, `top`, `pairL`, and `pairR`, and equality is decidable, so `pairL bot` and `pairR bot` are two tokens where clause (2) has the single ordered pair \((\Delta,\Delta)\). `VCon` is constantly true (`VCon_any`), and `vSystem` takes that as consistency, so the subset, singleton, and entailment-consistency axioms hold outright. `VEnt` has seven constructors: `bot` with no hypothesis, which is (3) for every finite set; `top_mem` and `top_both`, which are the two disjuncts of (4); `pairL_top` and `pairL_proj`, which are (5); and `pairR_top` and `pairR_proj`, which are (6). `VEnt_of_ent_top` shows that a set entailing `top` entails every token, by matching the conclusion and, for a pair, either using membership of `top` or recursing into the corresponding projection. `not_VEnt_empty_top` inducts on a derivation and rewrites an empty projection by `vPairLFinset_empty`. `not_VEnt_singleton_ne_top` cases on the token, rejects `top_mem` by the singleton, and rejects `top_both` because the opposite projection is empty, so the only singleton equivalent to \(\nabla\) is \(\{\nabla\}\). `VEnt_of_mem` inducts on the token and uses `mem_vPairLFinset` and `mem_vPairRFinset`. `VEnt_trans` inducts on the derivation: `bot` is immediate; `top_mem` is the entailment of that member; `top_both` pushes the member-entailments through `VEnt_pairLFinset_of_EntSet` and `VEnt_pairRFinset_of_EntSet`, invoking `VEnt_of_ent_top` when `top` appears in the stronger set, and otherwise applies the inductive hypotheses; a left pair derived from `top` is the rogue-top consequence, and a left pair derived by projection either becomes `pairL_top` or applies the inductive hypothesis, with the two right-pair cases symmetric. `UToken` is the subtype of `VToken` singled out by `IsUToken`, that is \(t\neq\mathrm{top}\), so `pairL top` and `pairR top` stay, and `uBot` is `bot`, which is (8). `UCon` is failure of `VEnt` of `top` on `uForgetFinset`, which is (9), and `UEnt` is that hypothesis together with the underlying `VEnt`, which is (10). `uSystem` discharges its six axioms by `UCon_subset` (`VEnt_mono` along `uForgetFinset_mono`), `UCon_singleton` (`not_VEnt_singleton_ne_top`), `UCon_insert_of_ent` (`not_VEnt_insert_top_of_ent`, from `VEnt_insert_top_imp` by induction on the inserted token and inversion of pair entailments), `UEnt_bot`, `UEnt_of_mem`, and `UEnt_trans` (`VEnt_trans` on forgetful images). `vPairLFinset` retains only `pairL` payloads and `vPairRFinset` only `pairR` payloads, so neither copy of \((\Delta,\Delta)\) contributes \(\Delta\) to both projections, whereas the product token \((\Delta,\Delta)\) contributes \(\Delta\) to both `fstFinset` and `sndFinset`. `vUnfold` sends `bot` to `prodBot`, which is \((\Delta,\Delta)\), sends both `pairL bot` and `pairR bot` to that same pair (`vUnfold_pairL`, `vUnfold_pairR`), and sends `top` to \((\nabla,\Delta)\). `vRhs_eq_product` is `rfl`, identifying `vRhs` with `productSystem vSystem vSystem`. `theorem_83` is `UCon_empty`, obtained from `not_VEnt_empty_top` by `uForgetFinset_empty`. The path reading, the Cantor-space isomorphism, the retract pair \((a,b)\), and the \(\lambda\)-model are not stated. **Theorem:** partial — `vSystem` and `uSystem` are the information systems of clauses (1)–(10), including the rogue-top property and the deletion of \(\nabla\), but \(V\) is not shown isomorphic to \(V\times V\), because `pairL bot` and `pairR bot` stay distinct while `vUnfold` collapses both with `bot` and sends \(\nabla\) to \((\nabla,\Delta)\), and `theorem_83` is only consistency of the empty set of \(U\). **Proof:** partial — the six axioms of \(V\) are the trivial consistency checks together with `VEnt_of_mem` and `VEnt_trans`, and the six axioms of \(U\) reduce to those by the forgetful image and `VEnt_insert_top_imp`, while the domain equation and the retract are not proved.
